Subject: A register function read from bytecode gets the compiler's variable area (recorded in the flag byte, checked against what the program names), not all of var_count

What this does
  - `regvm_wire_decode` set every blob function's `regvm_var_count` to `var_count`: the compiler's promotion map is not serialized, and an unpromoted frame has to fill and release every variable slot. The compiler, though, promotes most locals to registers, so a function compiled from source has `regvm_var_count` 0 (richards, deltablue: 0 for nearly every function) and its frame has no variable area at all.
  - The writer records `regvm_var_count` in the register program's flag byte (bit 0 is the flag; bits 1-7 hold the area plus one, 0 meaning "not recorded"; recorded for functions with at most 126 variables). The reader takes the recorded value when it is at least the area the program and its inner closures need and at most `var_count`, else the load fails ("invalid register variable area"). Without a recorded value (a blob from an older writer, the checked-in builtin blobs, more than 126 variables) it derives the area: one past the highest variable slot the decoded program names (the variable cases of `regvm_slot_operands_ok`, factored as `regvm_var_slot_hi`), or one past the highest slot an inner closure captures (`JS_CLOSURE_LOCAL` entries of the function's constant-pool children; a captured local is never promoted and only the capturing child names it). The `arguments` variable (never promoted) counts. A direct eval (`ROP_eval`/`ROP_apply_eval`) or more than 255 slots keeps all of `var_count`. `regvm_slot_operands_ok` now also bounds the implicit-slot forms `put_local_xfer_s0..3` and `get_local_field_0..3` by `var_count` (it did not).
  - `JS_TestRegvmVarCountOfChild` (register builds): a test hook returning a nested function's `regvm_var_count`, so a test can compare a function compiled from source with the same function read back.

Why we need it
  F52: the same register program loaded from bytecode ran 6-10% slower than from source (octane richards -5.4..-6.2%, deltablue -9.2..-10.0%; the kit deltablue executes 12% more instructions, 2111M against 1885M, with identical dynamic opcode counts). Cause: every call filled and released all `var_count` variable slots and a light frame was bigger (deltablue's function at line 1000: light_need 400 against 304). After the change the bytecode run executes 1855M instructions against 1885M from source; across 13,510 functions of the differential corpora, the kit and the Octane corpus, the source-compiled and the read-back functions agree on every call-descriptor and frame field (ready, light, fast, threaded, pic, vc, regs, ctl, frame/light need, scan, args_captured, var_refs, argv_safe) except the entry fill list (source: precise; a blob always fills every register, the safe superset) and `regvm_var_count` of two functions with 301 variables, a field no consumer reads above 255.

Soundness
  A slot the frame does not reserve must never be touched. The program touches variable slots only through the instructions `regvm_slot_operands_ok` already bounds by `var_count` (the same list, now also taken as the high-water mark), and an inner closure reaches a parent's slot only through its `JS_CLOSURE_LOCAL` descriptor; the area covers both. Direct eval, which reads variables by name, keeps the full area. The derivation uses only validated operands of the decoded program, so a hostile blob cannot make it smaller than what the program names.
